    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That \"blended approach\" sounds good in theory, but when you look at the actual data, isn't it true that the enforcement side is basically the only way to ensure the stability needed for those economic programs to even work? It seems like without the security piece being the absolute priority, the whole system just collapses. Can you defend the idea that prioritizing humanitarian pathways is actually a \"stable\" strategy for the economy?",
